Log the ground-lock RELEASE, which the gate probe could not see.        (SIM-27)

MEASUREMENT ONLY. Adds no behaviour. REQUIRES 0008 (it uses that patch's sim27Log and its
per-gate throttle enum); apply 0008 first.

WHY THIS EXISTS. 0008 answered "which acquisition gate rejects the landing surface" and the
answer was read too broadly. Across six instrumented splits, ONE rejected on the collision
normal (gate B, |normal.z| ~ 0.0003, no lock) and FIVE passed gate B with |normal.z| = 1.00000,
ENGAGED the ground lock, and split anyway. So on the dominant path the lock is acquired and the
vehicle still descends -- which means the fault is downstream of acquisition, where nothing was
being logged.

The suspect is right there in getNextKinematicsNoCollision:

    if (body.isGrounded()) {
        float external_force_magnitude = body_wrench.force.squaredNorm();
        Vector3r weight = body.getMass() * body.getEnvironment().getState().gravity;
        float weight_magnitude = weight.squaredNorm();
        if (external_force_magnitude >= weight_magnitude)
            body.setGrounded(false);          // <- upstream: "Losing ground lock due to body_wrench"

The lock holds only while the net external force stays BELOW the vehicle's weight. During
AUTO.LAND the rotors sit at very nearly hover thrust -- which is, by definition, about equal to
weight. So this comparison sits exactly on its own boundary at the one moment it has to hold,
and if it tips, the body is released back into free integration while the Unreal actor stays
blocked by the geometry it landed on. That would grow a split at precisely MPC_LAND_SPEED, which
is what every failing trace shows.

  gate=D-LOCK-HELD      every tick the body is grounded: force^2, weight^2, their ratio,
                        and whether this tick releases
  gate=E-LOCK-RELEASED  the release actually taken, with the descent rate at that moment

If D shows ratio hovering at ~1.0 and E fires repeatedly through the descent, the acquisition
gates were never the fault and the fix belongs in this release test. If E never fires, the lock
is being lost somewhere else and this rules out the obvious place.

EVERY LINE NAMES ITS BODY, and the first version of this probe is why. It printed
force2=0.0000 for 171 s straight, across a mission the aircraft visibly flew -- and a body under
rotor thrust cannot report zero wrench. The lines were describing some other body in the physics
world, and with no identity in them there was no way to tell which. Position, velocity and mass
are printed for the same reason: they say whether the body being logged is the one that
descended. An instrument that cannot say what it measured produces confident nonsense.

Read with:  docker logs sim-unreal 2>&1 | grep -E "gate=[DE]"

